VIM››vim [file]››A compact, modal, single-file editor.›It uses the proven EOS full-screen›text›buffer, renderer and CIO file paths›while providing NORMAL and INSERT›modes.›The status line begins with N or I for›the current mode.››NORMAL commands include arrows, i, a,›o, x, dd, yy, p, u, 0, $, gg, G,›literal / search, n, and : commands.›INSERT accepts text, Tab, Return and›Backspace; Escape returns to NORMAL.›Cursor keys move without leaving›INSERT;›typing continues at the new position,›including on another existing line.›Undo reverses the most recent bounded›edit segment.››Commands are :w, :w file, :q, :q!,›:wq, :e file, and :e! file. Existing›files require Y confirmation. Failed›loads leave the text and filename›unchanged; failed writes keep the›changed flag set. Replacement is›checked›but not crash-atomic, so keep backups›on unreliable media.››VIM uses 22 editing rows and 255-byte›logical lines. It accepts printable›ASCII, tabs, and consistently ATASCII,›LF, or CRLF newlines. LF/CRLF files›retain their style; new files use LF.›Mixed newlines, NUL/control bytes, and›longer lines are rejected without›changing the buffer.››On the tested 64K 800XL configuration,›24,832 bytes of 127-byte lines load;›24,976 bytes is safely refused.›Capacity varies slightly with the›final line.›VIM.COM is 10,577 bytes including›relocation data (8,686 bytes linked›code›and static workspace), above the›aspirational 8 KiB target. VIM is›transient,›so this does not enlarge the resident›kernel.››The legacy non-modal ed remains on the›Toolkit disk.›